__d(function(A,J,I,E,k,C,g){k.exports=[{slug:"how-to-use",title:"What is Gymfolio",icon:"book-outline",order:0,sections:[{heading:"What is Gymfolio?",body:"Gymfolio is a exercise tracker that can help you improve strength and endurance by recording your gym sessions. Progressive overload is the most important principle when it comes to improving at the gym and loosing weight, and Gymfolio is designed with this in mind. \n\nI've tried other gym tracker apps and found they did too much, weren't simple enough to navigate or tried to steer me into doing what they though was right for me. I've been using pen and paper until I decided to create this app. Zero distractions, versatile for any kind of workout and a breeze to set up and navigate. \n \nThe app comes with many features but never forces you to use all of them. Check out the settings to tailor the app to your liking and even disable some features."},{heading:"What Gymfolio isn't?",body:"Gymfolio isn't going to tell you what the best exercises or workout for you is. The apps will come with preset exercise and workouts for beginners to get started but if users want to get the best results, doing their own research or getting a Personal Trainer is the best option.\n\nThe Tips and Guides in the app should only server as topics that users should look into themselves. What might work for one person may not work for another so it is important to broaden knowledge and not to be afraid to change things up from time to time."}],id:"6ab900241b0d6a539cf699b8"},{slug:"how-to-use-gymfolio-rnt8",title:"How to use Gymfolio.",icon:"bulb-outline",sections:[{heading:"Create your exercise Library.",body:"From the homepage, go to \"Exercises\" and then to \"Create Exercise\". From there, enter the details of your exercise. Name, muscle group, photo (optional) and what you want to track. You can also choose if you want to track warmup sets and mini sets. Once you created your exercise, it will appear on the \"Exercise\" page.\n\nYou can combine 2 or more exercises in \"Multi Sets\". Multi sets are is where you can create Super sets, Giant sets and Circuits.\n\nWhen you think you have enough exercises, you can create a workout.",image:"data:image/png;base64,"},{heading:"Creating workouts.",body:"In \"Workouts\" create a workout by filtering throught the exercises and multi sets you created. Onces you created your workout, it will appear on the \"workouts\" page. Click on the workout to get to the \"Preform Workout\" page.\n\nYou can reorder the exercise as you go and once you log your workout, the list order will be saved. This is handy if you like to change up the order of your workouts.\n\nYou don't have complete every exercise to log your workout and you can add exercises as you go. After logging your workout you'll be taken to the workout progress page and look at that workout's history.",image:null},{heading:"Creating HIIT.",body:"You can also add HIIT exercises to you workouts. Simply create a HIIT by setting the intervals length. Once you create you HIIT, you can press on it on the \"HIIT\" page. You'll get a chance to edit your HIIT before you begin. Start your HIIT and listen out for the alarms that signals interval changes.",image:null},{heading:"Progress and History",body:"After completing a workout, you'll be brought to the \"Progress\" page where you can look through your workout history and navigate to each individual \"Exercise Progress\" page. Here is where your performance is tracked. You can also navigate to this by pressing on the blue button on an exercise card in \"Exercises\".",image:null},{heading:"Calendar",body:"The calendar automatically tracks when you do a workout. It can also be used to help track your weight and to set up \"Commitment Periods\" to track or remind yourself to diet, bulk or try new workout programs/experiment.",image:null},{heading:"Stretches",body:"Here you find a library of useful stretches or you can add your own.",image:null},{heading:"TDEE",body:"TDEE stands for Total Daily Energy Expenditure. Its a useful calculator to help you with your dietary goals.",image:null}],order:1,id:"6ab9095c1b0d6a539cf699bd"},{slug:"beginner-tips",title:"Starting at the Gym.",icon:"rocket-outline",order:2,sections:[{heading:"Ease yourself in.",body:"When you start off, you'll be using muscles that you probably hardly ever use. Even if you can bench press, say 3 sets of 10 reps with 40kgs, your muscles might not be ready to be pushed to their max. Start off by using around 70% of you full strength. This will get your muscles use to the movements, reduce the chance of injury and allow your muscles to recover a little easier and feel less sore. After your gym sessions, make sure to stretch. Normally 5 minutes after a session, but when you start off first, aim for 10 minutes after your first few sessions."},{heading:"How to start?",body:"A full body workout is the most beginner friendly way to start at the gym. By working all the major muscle groups (legs, arms, shoulder, chest, back and core) we can quickly get your body adjusted to weight training. There is already 2 full body workouts premade in the \"workout\" page. You can go ahead and alternate between these 2 and when your ready, you can edit them, create new full bodies or do some research into other types of workouts like Upper/Lower split or Push/Pull/Legs."},{heading:"Prep for your workout.",body:"Hydrate well before and after your workout and everyday. Aim for 2 Litters of water a day. Have a good meal 2-3 hours before a workout. Carbs are your fuel for your workout, lean protein like chicken breast contain nutrients that help muscle repair and recovery. Eat a snack that can digest easily 30 minutes before a workout to get a boost of energy as you start. Fruit, in particular, bananas are great for this\n\nFirst thing you want to before we lift weights is a cardio warm up. A 3-5 minute treadmill run/jog will get your heart going and blood pumping to your muscles, preparing your body and reducing your chance of injury. The only time you need to do a stretching exercise is if your muscles feel tight. Do dynamic stretching (moving parts of your body through a full range of motion) to prepare your muscles for heavy lifting."},{heading:"Compound lifts.",body:"Compound lifts are exercise that use multiple joints and targets a few muscles groups. You should do these at the start of you workout center your workout around these exercises as they are more effective at building muscle and burning calories. They are also more time effective and tire you out quicker. A good example are squats. They uses the hip and knee joints and targets the quads, glutes, hamstrings, and core. The Barbell squat is the most effective squat because it helps train your balance but also the most difficult. Squat Machine are a easier alternative. The Goblet squat is a great middle ground.",image:"data:image/png;base64,"},{heading:"Isolated exercises.",body:"Isolated exercises only use one joint and targets one muscle group at a time. These are for if you want to targeting one muscle that compound exercises don't stimulate enough. It's also safer to push to failure, which is the best way to stimulate growth. Bicep Curls is and example of and isolated exercise.",image:"data:image/png;base64,"},{heading:"Pick your rep range.",body:"As a beginner, you should work anywhere between 6 to 12 reps. This is known as the Hypertrophy range, the sweet spot for strength endurance and muscle growth. If you're more interested in building strength, between 1-6 reps. Over 12 reps and you building more endurance. \n\nOn the 3rd set, you should push yourself. If you are going to failure or close to failure, do so safely by using a spotter or safety equipment. If you can do 2 sets of 10 reps and on your 3rd set you do 12 reps, next session you aim for 3 sets of 11. Keep doing this until you get to the top of your rep range and then increase the weight and repeat the process. This is the most effective way to progressively overload.",image:"data:image/png;base64,"},{heading:"Rest time.",body:"Rest time depends on you, your goals and the exercise you are doing. The simplest rule to follow is the bigger the movement, the longer the rest. For a compound movement like a squat, you should rest for 2-3 minutes between sets. For an isolated exercise like bicep curls 1-2 minutes. Experiment and see what works for you."},{heading:"Form is Important.",body:"Look online when trying a new exercise to learn how to do them properly. Without good form, your exercise wont be as effective and you risk injuring yourself. Your better off reducing weight than compromising your form.",image:"data:image/png;base64,"},{heading:"Post workout.",body:"Do some cool down cardio. 3-5 minutes of walking or brisk walk will do. Hydrate, a good balanced meal and plenty of stretching. Protein after a workout is important to build muscle and aid in recovery. \n\nAnd of course, get recommended amount of sleep. Some people can get away with 7 hours, some need 9, everyone is different."},{heading:"Its not a sprint, its a marathon.",body:"Consistently is key. Find that balance where you feel like going to the gym and it doesn't feel like a chore. It's ok to miss a session here and there as long it doesn't become a habit. If it does, maybe because you feel like you don't have the energy or the time, change up your workout or routine. As you fitness increase and you get into gym culture more, you will find it more enjoyable and rewarding."}],id:"6ab900241b0d6a539cf699b9"},{slug:"diet-exercise",title:"Diet",icon:"nutrition-outline",order:3,sections:[{heading:"What is your Goal?",body:"Click on TDEE on the home page and enter your details. It will show you a simple table of how much calories you should be eating to achieve you goals. Best thing to do is look at you calorie maintenance and aim for lower or higher, depending on your goal and then adjust you current eating habits depending on that goal.\n\nTracking calories can sometimes feel like a chore, I suggest doing it for a few weeks until you have a good grasp on the calories of the foods you eat. Every so often, weigh yourself or look at past photos of yourself and decide yourself if you need to change your diet."},{heading:"Understand metabolism.",body:"Your metabolism is the process your body uses to turn food into energy and keep you functioning. The calories you eat can be used straight away, stored for later, or used to build and repair tissue.\n\nYour body gets energy mainly from carbohydrates and fats, while protein is particularly important for building and repairing muscle. When you consistently eat more energy than your body uses, the excess can contribute to fat gain. When you consistently eat less, your body uses stored energy, including body fat, to make up the difference.\n\nA simple way to think about it:\n\nFood provides energy\nYour body uses energy all day, not just during exercise\nMuscle requires energy to maintain and use\nConsistently eating excess calories can lead to fat gain\nEating fewer calories than you use can lead to fat loss\nBody fat isn't necessarily a bad thing. It stores energy, protects your organs, helps regulate hormones and supports the absorption of certain vitamins.\n\nYou don't need to fully understand metabolism to manage your weight. The main concept to remember is energy balance, how much energy you consume compared with how much your body uses."},{heading:"Protein",body:"Lean meats like chicken breast are an excellent source of high-quality protein, providing plenty of protein without adding many extra calories. However, variety is important, so try to get protein from a range of foods such as fish, eggs, dairy, meat and plant-based sources.\n\nProtein powders can be a convenient way to reach your target, but they're not necessary. Think of them as a supplement to your normal diet rather than a replacement for nutritious foods.\n\nBeans, especially lentils are great source of protein due to being high in fiber and very filling."}],id:"6ab900241b0d6a539cf699ba"},{slug:"optimize-workouts",title:"More Topics",icon:"speedometer-outline",order:4,sections:[{heading:"Progressive Overload",body:"Progressive overload means gradually making your training harder as you improve. This could mean adding weight, reps, volume, range of motion or simply improving your control and form.\n\nYou don't need to improve every session. Look at your progress over weeks and months rather than worrying about individual workouts.\n\nAs your body adapts, consider increasing the challenge. Giving your body something new to adapt to is what keeps you progressing.\n\nResearch yourself: Progressive overload, training volume and double progression."},{heading:"Reps in Reserve (RIR)",body:"RIR is a way of judging how hard a set was. If you finish and could have completed another 2 good reps, that's roughly 2 RIR.\n\nUsing RIR allows you to train hard without taking every set to failure. Getting close to failure can stimulate muscle growth without always needing to reach it.\n\nJudging RIR takes practice. The more familiar you become with an exercise, the better you'll get at estimating how many reps you have left.\n\nResearch yourself: RIR, RPE and proximity to failure."},{heading:"Training to Failure",body:"Training to failure means continuing until you can't complete another full rep with good form. It can be useful, but it isn't necessary for every set.\n\nTraining close to failure can produce great results while potentially creating less fatigue. How close you go can depend on the exercise and your goals.\n\nFailure on a bicep curl is very different from failure on a heavy squat. Consider the risk of the exercise before deciding how far to push yourself.\n\nResearch yourself: Training to failure, proximity to failure and fatigue management."},{heading:"Rep Ranges",body:"You've probably heard of different rep ranges for strength, muscle growth and endurance. While these ranges are useful guidelines, there aren't strict boundaries between them \u2014 you can build muscle and strength across a variety of rep ranges.\n\nDifferent exercises may also feel better at different ranges. Experiment with heavier and lighter sets and see what works for your goals and the exercise you're performing.\n\nResearch yourself: Rep ranges, hypertrophy, strength training and muscular endurance."},{heading:"Rest Times",body:"How long you rest can affect how well you perform your next set. Short rests may save time, but longer rests can allow you to recover and maintain more reps or weight, particularly during demanding exercises.\n\nThere's no perfect rest time for every exercise. Try different rest periods and pay attention to whether you're actually ready to perform your next set well.\n\nResearch yourself: Rest intervals, fatigue and strength recovery."},{heading:"Warm-Up Sets",body:"Warm-up sets prepare you for a heavy exercise by practising the movement with lighter weights. Gradually increase the weight as you approach your working sets, without doing so many reps that you tire yourself out.\n\nThe heavier or more demanding the exercise, the more useful warm-up sets can become. There's no single warm-up routine that works for everything, so learn what preparation you need for different exercises.\n\nResearch yourself: Warm-up sets, ramp-up sets and working sets."},{heading:"Deloading",body:"A deload is a planned period where you temporarily reduce how much or how hard you train. It can give you a break from accumulated fatigue without completely abandoning your routine.\n\nDeloading isn't something everyone needs on a strict schedule, and research into its benefits is still developing. Pay attention to your performance and recovery rather than assuming you need one every few weeks.\n\nResearch yourself: Deloading, fatigue management and recovery."},{heading:"Exercise Order",body:"The exercises you do first are generally performed when you're freshest, so exercise order can reflect your priorities. If improving your bench press matters most, for example, consider putting it near the beginning.\n\nYou don't always have to do compound exercises first. Research suggests strength improvements tend to favour exercises performed earlier in the workout.\n\nResearch yourself: Exercise order, exercise priority and pre-exhaustion."},{heading:"Range of Motion",body:"Range of motion (ROM) is how far you move through an exercise. A deeper squat, for example, uses a greater ROM than a partial squat.\n\nFull ROM is a good general starting point, but partial reps can also have uses depending on your goals and the exercise. Don't sacrifice safe, controlled technique simply to achieve more range.\n\nResearch yourself: Range of motion, partial reps and lengthened partials."},{heading:"Rep Tempo",body:"Tempo describes how quickly you perform each part of a rep. Slowing down can help you control the movement and become more aware of your technique, but slower doesn't automatically mean better.\n\nDifferent tempos can be useful for different exercises and goals. Try controlling the weight rather than simply trying to make every repetition as slow as possible.\n\nResearch yourself: Rep tempo, eccentric training and time under tension."},{heading:"Volume vs Intensity",body:"Training volume describes how much work you're doing, while intensity can describe the load you're lifting or how difficult your sets are. Both can affect your progress.\n\nDoing more isn't always better. Your training needs to be challenging enough to create progress while still allowing you to recover between sessions.\n\nResearch yourself: Training volume, intensity, weekly sets and recovery."},{heading:"Training Frequency",body:"Training frequency is simply how often you train. You might train a particular muscle once, twice or several times each week depending on your routine.\n\nMore frequent training isn't automatically better. How you distribute your overall training volume across the week can be just as important.\n\nResearch yourself: Training frequency, weekly volume and workout splits."},{heading:"Supersets & Multi Sets",body:"Supersets involve performing exercises back-to-back with little rest. They're a great way to save time and fit more training into a shorter workout, although they can also make the workout more tiring.\n\nThere are different ways to combine exercises, such as training opposing muscles or the same muscle twice. Experiment with Multi Sets and find combinations that suit your workout.\n\nResearch yourself: Supersets, antagonist supersets, circuits and giant sets."},{heading:"Mind-Muscle Connection",body:"The mind-muscle connection means deliberately focusing on the muscle you're trying to train. There is evidence that this can influence muscle activation in some circumstances, particularly with lighter loads.\n\nFeeling a muscle working can be useful, but don't let it become your only measure of a good exercise. Good technique and measurable progress still matter.\n\nResearch yourself: Mind-muscle connection, internal focus and muscle activation."},{heading:"Free Weights vs Machines",body:"Free weights require you to control and stabilize the weight, while machines provide a fixed or guided movement. Neither is automatically better than the other.\n\nBoth can build muscle and strength effectively. Your goals, experience, comfort and the particular exercise should help determine which you use.\n\nResearch yourself: Free weights vs machines, stability and exercise selection."},{heading:"Variation vs Consistency",body:"Trying new exercises can keep training interesting and expose your muscles to different movements. However, constantly changing exercises can make it harder to measure whether you're actually progressing.\n\nGive exercises enough time to improve at them, but don't be afraid to make changes when there's a good reason. Variation should have a purpose.\n\nResearch yourself: Exercise variation, exercise selection and \"muscle confusion.\""},{heading:"Tracking Your Progress",body:"Progress isn't just adding more weight. More reps, better technique, improved range of motion and better performance with the same weight can all show improvement.\n\nDon't judge yourself from one bad workout. Look at your training history over weeks and months to get a better picture of whether you're progressing.\n\nResearch yourself: Progressive overload, performance trends and training logs."},{heading:"Does Soreness Mean Growth?",body:"Muscle soreness after training, often called DOMS, is common after unfamiliar or particularly demanding exercise. But being sore doesn't necessarily mean you've had a better workout.\n\nLikewise, not being sore doesn't mean your workout didn't work. Treat soreness as something your body experiences rather than a score for how successful your workout was.\n\nResearch yourself: DOMS, muscle damage, recovery and hypertrophy."},{heading:"Are Compound Lifts Essential?",body:"Squats, bench presses and deadlifts are excellent exercises, but you don't have to perform specific lifts to make progress. There are usually several ways to train the same muscles.\n\nChoose exercises that suit your goals, body and available equipment. Don't feel that an exercise is mandatory simply because it's popular.\n\nResearch yourself: Compound exercises, exercise selection and movement patterns."},{heading:"Should You Change Your Workout?",body:"You don't need to constantly change your workout to \"confuse\" your muscles. Keeping exercises consistent gives you the opportunity to practice them and properly measure your progress.\n\nThat doesn't mean you should never change anything. New exercises can address weaknesses, prevent boredom or simply suit you better as your goals change.\n\nResearch yourself: Muscle confusion, exercise variation and training programs."}],id:"6ab900241b0d6a539cf699bb"},{id:"0369e93733e44c638452b323",slug:"privacy-policy",title:"Privacy Policy",order:5,sections:[{heading:"Privacy Policy for Gymfolio",body:"Effective Date: 2 October 2026\n\nGymfolio respects your privacy. This Privacy Policy explains how information is handled when you use the Gymfolio mobile application."},{heading:"Information We Collect",body:"Gymfolio does not collect, transmit, sell, share, or store personal information about its users on external servers.\n\nYou are not required to provide personal information such as your name, email address, phone number, location, or other identifying information in order to use the app."},{heading:"Workout and Fitness Data",body:"Information you enter into Gymfolio, such as exercises, workouts, sets, repetitions, weights, times, distances, notes, or other fitness-related information, is used only to provide the app's functionality.\n\nGymfolio does not collect or transmit this information to the developer or to external servers.\n\nWhere information is stored on your device, it remains under your control and may be removed by deleting the relevant information within the app or by uninstalling the application."},{heading:"Third-Party Data Sharing",body:"Gymfolio does not sell, rent, or share user information with advertisers, data brokers, or other third parties."},{heading:"Analytics and Advertising",body:"Gymfolio does not use user data for behavioural advertising, profiling, or marketing purposes.\n\nGymfolio does not track users across other applications or websites."},{heading:"Permissions",body:"Gymfolio may request device permissions where required for a particular feature. Any permission requested by the app is used only to provide that feature and is not used to collect personal information for the developer.\n\nYou can manage application permissions through your device's settings."},{heading:"Children's Privacy",body:"Gymfolio does not knowingly collect personal information from children.\n\nBecause Gymfolio does not collect personal information from its users, it does not intentionally gather, store, or share information relating to children."},{heading:"Data Security",body:"Because Gymfolio does not transmit or store personal user information on external servers, the developer does not maintain a central database containing users' personal information.\n\nUsers are responsible for maintaining the security of their own device and any information stored locally on it."},{heading:"Changes to This Privacy Policy",body:"This Privacy Policy may be updated occasionally to reflect changes to Gymfolio or its functionality.\n\nAny changes will be published on the page where this Privacy Policy is made available. The effective date at the top of the policy will be updated when significant changes are made."},{heading:"Contact",body:"If you have questions about this Privacy Policy or Gymfolio's privacy practices, you can contact the developer at:\n\nEmail: rorywhelan8@gmail.com"}]}]},2479,[]);
